          "documents": [
            {
              "type": "History and physical note",
              "title": "New Patient Visit",
              "author": "PCP",
              "date": "2019-08-12T10:30",
              "body": "47F transferring in. Her old PCP is retiring and she wanted somewhere she could reach by text. Records requested.\n\nPMH\n- Hep C, genotype 1a, found 2011 on routine labs. Started an interferon-based regimen around 2012 and stopped partway through. Never re-treated.\n- HTN, on HCTZ 25 for a few years. Checks at the pharmacy machine occasionally, usually 130s.\n- Alcohol use disorder: drank daily, heavily, for more than ten years. Quit early 2018, no relapse. Not in a program now; says she doesn't need one.\nPSH: lap chole 2014 (age 42).\nMeds: HCTZ 25 mg daily.\nAllergies: none known.\nFH: mother HTN, breast cancer at 65. Father had cirrhosis from drinking. Maternal aunt had lung cancer and never smoked. Brother has type 2 DM.\nSH: office administrator. Single. Never smoked, no secondhand exposure. No alcohol since 2018. No drugs.\n\nROS: no fatigue, no jaundice, no abdominal swelling, no headaches, no chest pain. Otherwise negative.\n\nExam: BP and vitals per flowsheet. Comfortable. Anicteric. No spider angiomata. Oropharynx clear. Neck supple, no adenopathy. Heart regular rate, no murmur. Lungs clear bilaterally. Abdomen soft, nontender, well-healed laparoscopic scars, liver edge not palpable, no splenomegaly, no fluid wave. No edema. Skin without rash.\n\nA/P\n1. Chronic hep C: CMP, CBC, HCV RNA quant today. Hep B sAg and sAb. Starting Twinrix series today since no record of hepatitis A or B vaccination; she'll come back in a month and at 6 months.\n2. HTN: a little high in the office today, pharmacy readings 130s. Continue HCTZ 25, recheck next visit.\n3. AUD in remission: AUDIT-C 0. Congratulated her on 18 months.\n4. Health maintenance: Tdap today. HIV screen with labs. Lipids, A1c (brother with DM), TSH. Last mammogram 2018 per patient, Pap 2017; will confirm on records. Colon screening to discuss at the physical next year.\nFollow up 6 months, sooner with lab issues."
            }
          ]
